#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069746} :activity/id #uuid "211f43a9-ed58-52eb-922b-5024799258b3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050159 :topic/id #uuid "32cf9e59-1dd8-51df-b1af-51ac52d78285" :topic/math-academy-id 415} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069988 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $x=f(y),$ the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d,$ as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/19/1927064dcef318f2c0490ff071532b024da30a328b2752c28e197171eeec6b5f.png)\n\nOne way of computing the volume of the solid is to divide the interval from $y=c$ to $y=d$ on the $y$-axis to get horizontal slices. This approach is called the **shell method**.\n\nConsider one such slice of height $\\Delta{y},$ as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $x$-axis, it forms a solid **shell** (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/25/2561448ab909594dda427b76860fcdda5147f8966fec9e20958d1c5fca11b536.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{y}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $y.$ The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{y}$ and its height is $x=f(y)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its width is $x=f(y)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{y},$ and its height is $2\\pi y$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/03/03030fb80f0aa51c637e6d39b4f763e8d7fc89abd111a012d3e560a952cfba99.png)\n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{shell}} & ≈{V}_{\\text{rec}} \\\\  & =2\\pi y\\cdot x\\cdot Δy \\\\  & =2\\pi yf(y)Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum over all the shells will approximate the total volume that we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{y} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}yx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y\\,f(y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6b39e027-cd9c-572b-9d98-e3b708e1b618" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5286 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "72547125-fabd-5779-a865-3e7942bbad99" :step/math-academy-id 643617 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068657 :question/id #uuid "29e835eb-531e-5241-911f-45e2d686b183" :question/math-academy-id "e-8853"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f9081-96b8-53f5-8881-b17bea5d0316" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531625}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"} :step/id #uuid "7a174fca-da25-58a3-b88f-d062ddd5cad6" :step/math-academy-id 643622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069989 :tutorial/content "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $x = f(y)$ about the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/1d/1d23dde2cdd557feb50f82f72f4ff09174d5fb9a0c94e7810a6c06acb95a13d0.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}yf(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine this volume using the disc method, we'd first need to express our curve as two separate functions of $x,$ one for the lower part and one for the upper part, as follows:\n\n![](images/d9/d957a8e263db8ddd8ad3e5fa37f63b0cdbb47e706c367350c3ff6b8ff90294ff.svg)\n\n![](images/f4/f416ab43297265aec4836eca2020d8f08976852cc025ad4d3a0766023187adef.png)\n\nThe required volume is equal to the difference between the volumes generated when the upper and lower curves are rotated about the $x$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" :tutorial/id #uuid "cb718bb7-7c09-555f-bd37-dadebd89ba90" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5292 :tutorial/title "Advantages of the Shell Method"} :step/id #uuid "4660fad3-185d-5d29-946d-ac581eb8d39f" :step/math-academy-id 643621 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068658 :question/id #uuid "77659797-1ecc-567e-b864-cfb39c21b0c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8856"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49361606-6120-5b9c-888f-0cdaf012f125" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531648}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"} :step/id #uuid "b93bd632-6fba-5317-ad2d-eb331be9e45c" :step/math-academy-id 643618 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069748}}] :activity/title "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region About the X-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068950}]}
